</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the noise robustness evaluation in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.2.3 Evaluation on Noise Robustness ‣ 6.2 Evaluation results ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ FunAudio-ASR Technical Report" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. It is evident that noise robust training (NRT) is crucial for industrial applications. In challenging environments such as dinner and supermarket settings, NRT brings over 30% relative improvement, as LLM-based ASR systems tend to generate hallucinated outputs under such complex acoustic conditions. Furthermore, RL further enhances the model’s noise robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.4 </span>Code-switching Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation, two test sets A and B are used to evaluate the effectiveness of the constructed code-switched training data (Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Code-switching ‣ 5 Production-oriented Optimization ‣ FunAudio-ASR Technical Report" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>). Test sets A and B are randomly selected from the
daily_dialogue_mixed_chinese_english_speech_tts dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">yiwu2, 2025</a>)</cite> and our in-house recordings, respectively. The results are shown in Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.2.4 Code-switching Evaluation ‣ 6.2 Evaluation results ‣ 6 Evaluation ‣ FunAudio-ASR Technical Report" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T5" class="ltx_table">
